---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/use-cases/email-design";
const title = "Shopify email design for readable product decisions";
const desc = "Build reusable templates around the action a reader needs to take, then review the actual rendered message and its product data.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "How should newsletter and lifecycle layouts differ?",
    "answer": "Give each template a clear job. A newsletter may introduce a story or collection; a lifecycle message should preserve the product and event context that made it relevant."
  },
  {
    "question": "How many products should an email show?",
    "answer": "Choose enough to support the decision without obscuring the primary action. There is no universal product count; inspect the complete mobile reading path and the relevance of each item."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should dark mode be reviewed?",
    "answer": "Inspect delivered examples in the clients your audience uses, including logos, transparent assets, text and buttons. An editor preview is one check, not proof of all client behavior."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does an accessible template establish legal compliance?",
    "answer": "No. Readable text, useful image descriptions and clear links are design practices. This guide does not assess legal obligations or certify compliance."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16"><a class="link" href="/use-cases">All use cases</a><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-6 text-xl">{desc}</p></section>
  <section class="container pb-16"><article class="card prose-shopify">
<h2>Give each template an information hierarchy</h2>
<p>Start with the reader's task: explore a collection, recognize an item left behind, find order information or learn how to use a purchase. Put the essential context and action ahead of supporting imagery. Keep marketing and order-service templates under separate ownership so a campaign redesign cannot accidentally hide tracking or order details.</p>
<p>Define a small set of template families with reusable header, body, product and footer modules. Record which fields are mandatory for each family. A cart module needs recognizable variant information and a useful destination; an editorial module needs a clear story and optional merchandising. Sharing brand assets does not require copying the same product grid into every message.</p>
<h2>Make product data part of design review</h2>
<p>A good-looking card with a wrong size, stale price or empty image is still a broken message. Specify fallback behavior for missing product images, long titles, multiple currencies, unavailable variants and absent personalization. Do not leave empty headings or greetings when data is missing. Review the longest plausible values as well as the polished sample record.</p>
<p>For a hypothetical keyboard catalog, a proposed layout could show the selected component and a short compatibility question with a link to detailed specifications. Avoid squeezing a full technical comparison into a narrow card. For a hypothetical pottery newsletter, a process photograph can support a studio story, while the product name and collection destination remain readable without that photograph. Neither example is a reported performance result.</p>
<h2>Review the reading path without relying on images</h2>
<p>Use live text for the central product explanation and action. Write image descriptions that convey useful information rather than repeating decorative filenames. Give links descriptive labels, preserve a sensible heading order and make adjacent links distinguishable. Check whether color alone carries an important distinction and whether text remains readable against its background.</p>
<p>Inspect narrow screens, enlarged text and image-blocked views. Ensure buttons and inline links are comfortably usable without overlapping targets. Read the plain-text alternative for meaning and destinations rather than treating it as a dump of tracking links. These are proposed review practices, not a legal or deliverability certification.</p>
<h2>Inspect delivered messages, including dark appearances</h2>
<p>Build a review matrix from the email clients and devices relevant to your audience and support history. Include light and dark appearances, images enabled and blocked, and the text alternative. Look for disappearing logos, low-contrast buttons, clipped copy, unexpected spacing and wide specification tables. Do not assume a background-color declaration controls every client.</p>
<p>Keep a reference example for each approved template and record the message version and client used for review. A screenshot is useful evidence for that case, not proof of every recipient's rendering. Repeat the relevant checks after changing the HTML, shared modules, product feed or sending platform. The proposed checks on this page have not been executed.</p>
<h2>Separate layout approval from audience approval</h2>
<p>Design review answers whether the message communicates accurately. Journey review answers whether this recipient should receive it now. A correct cart card does not prove a completed purchase will exit the queue. Keep the <a href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">recovery contract</a> and <a href="/use-cases/browse-abandonment">browse-interest policy</a> alongside template requirements, with different owners where needed.</p>
<p>Similarly, a readable incentive does not establish eligibility. Check the visible terms, destination and fallback when a recipient or product is excluded. Preserve essential order-service information when modifying shared branding. This page offers no jurisdiction-specific rules about promotional content in operational mail.</p>
<h2>Control template versions and exceptions</h2>
<p>Name templates by purpose and retain a change owner, approval date and rollback copy. Record which workflows use a shared module before editing it. A seasonal clone should have an expiry or explicit promotion to the standard template; otherwise outdated offers and decorative assets can survive into routine journeys.</p>
<p>Ask the designer and operator to review a proposed change together: can they update a product module without breaking conditional branches, export the relevant HTML, and restore an approved version? Keep exception notes for custom code and integration-dependent blocks. A library of reusable modules is useful only if someone owns their behavior.</p>
<h2>Evaluate an editor with your actual content</h2>
<p>Use the <a href="/apps">catalog</a> to shortlist Sequenzy and other tools, then provide the same template brief to each. Request demonstrations of product-field fallbacks, long titles, conditional sections, mobile reading, text alternatives and version recovery. Record the intended plan, external dependencies and custom work. No vendor ranking or current feature certification is implied here.</p>
<p>Choose based on the editing job: a small team maintaining a few simple layouts has different needs from a brand team coordinating many conditional templates. Compare operator effort and maintenance risk using the same content change, rather than choosing from gallery thumbnails or unsupported claims about design-led revenue lifts.</p>
<h2>Measure a design change against its job</h2>
<p>Before comparing variants, name the decision the change is meant to improve and hold the audience and offer comparable where feasible. Review destination success, product understanding and support feedback alongside clicks and orders. A change in attributed revenue alone cannot isolate the effect of layout. Use <a href="/use-cases/analytics">analytics</a> for measurement definitions.</p>
<p>Retain a defect log with the template version, missing field or rendering condition, affected path and correction owner. Prioritize faults that hide the next action or misstate product information. Avoid replacing a useful explanation merely to reduce message length or enforce an arbitrary product-card count.</p>
  </article></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
